<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="59014" href="schedule/2014/fall/NRES/499/59014.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2014" href="schedule/2014.xml">2014</calendarYear><term id="120148" href="schedule/2014/fall.xml">Fall 2014</term><subject id="NRES" href="schedule/2014/fall/NRES.xml">Natural Resources and Environmental Sciences</subject><course id="499" href="schedule/2014/fall/NRES/499.xml">Special Topics</course></parents><sectionNumber>WEM</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Wetland Ecology &amp; Management</sectionTitle><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Wetlands are important ecosystems that support high biodiversity and provide numerous benefits to society.  This course provides a comprehensive examination of wetland science, management and governance.   Lectures, readings and class discussions will focus on the structure and processes of wetland ecosystems, wetland conservation and management, and U.S. wetlands policy.  Special emphasis will be placed on the application of wetland science to policy, restoration and monitoring.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2014-08-25Z</startDate><endDate>2014-12-10Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>02:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MWF    </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>W115</roomNumber><buildingName>Turner Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Matthews" firstName="J">Matthews, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>